 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.06666666666666667,
   "dur": 3.439,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Which has more pigment cells? Count them, row by row."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.5,
   "dur": 11.053,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "In the bottom row of skin, each pigment cell feeds melanin to a few dozen neighbours. Count them across these skins, and the number comes out about the same."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 16.633333333333333,
   "dur": 8.688,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "What changes is the work each one does. In deeper skin, they make more of the dark melanin, in larger grains, handed out one by one."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 26.433333333333334,
   "dur": 10.126,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "It's easy to assume darker skin simply has more of them. Nina Jablonski and George Chaplin set skin colour beside sunlight, all around the world, and the two matched."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 39.5,
   "dur": 8.725,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Where sunlight is fierce, darker skin guards folate, which healthy pregnancies need. Where it's weak, lighter skin lets enough through to make vitamin D."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 52.1,
   "dur": 6.928,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "And on any one patch of skin, the pigment cells sit about a third of a paper's thickness apart."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 60.03333333333333,
   "dur": 5.738,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "So, which has more? None of them. Same cells, every colour, each answering its own sky."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 67.03333333333333,
   "dur": 3.754,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: why do we still get goosebumps, with almost no fur?"
  }
 ]
}